"""
Flettner-rotor (rotating-cylinder Magnus-effect) propulsor.

A vertical spinning cylinder mounted on the ship. Cross-wind on the rotating
surface develops a Magnus lift force perpendicular to the apparent wind, plus a
larger-than-static cylinder drag. Both coefficients depend on the dimensionless
spin ratio `ξ = |ω|·R / U_app`.

The (Cl(ξ), Cd(ξ)) curves are sourced from a WaterLily.jl CFD characterization
of a 2D rotating cylinder in cross-flow — see
`scripts/run_waterlily_flettner.jl`. That script runs an offline WaterLily sweep
over ξ ∈ [0, 6] and writes `assets/flettner_coeffs.csv`, which this component
reads at simulation time via two `BlockComponents.Tables.Interpolation` blocks.
Re-running the script and refreshing the CSV is the way to substitute newer /
higher-resolution CFD data without touching this `.dyad` file.

Sign convention of the CSV: the table stores `WaterLily.total_force` directly,
which (per `WaterLily/src/Metrics.jl::pressure_force`) integrates `p·n̂_out` —
the force the **body exerts on the fluid**. The conventional aerodynamic
force-on-body coefficients are the negation of that. We negate at the point of
use below (`Cl_eng = -Cl_table`, `Cd_eng = -Cd_table`), so the CSV stays as raw
WaterLily output (easy to re-generate / cross-check) and the in-Dyad force
algebra speaks the standard aerodynamic convention (Cl > 0 means lift in +n̂
perpendicular-to-wind, Cd > 0 means drag along the wind).

Why a precomputed table and not live per-step CFD: a single WaterLily step at
moderate resolution costs many ms, and a Dyad transient solver calls the rhs
hundreds of times per simulated second — running a 2D CFD at every rhs
evaluation across a 2200-s ship simulation is many orders of magnitude too
slow. Industrial Flettner designs (Norsepower, Maranet) use the same
"characterize-offline, look up live" pattern.

Interface (matches `WingSail.dyad` for drop-in substitution):
- `Omega_Order` — commanded rotor angular velocity [rad/s]. Sign chooses which
  way the lift points (positive ω = CCW from above → lift to port of the wind).
- `WindSpeedX_in`, `WindSpeedY_in` — apparent wind velocity components in the
  ship's body frame (same convention as `WingSail`).
- `frame_a` — `Frame2D` mount point on the hull (PlanarMechanics).

Outputs:
- `Omega` — actual spin rate after `MaxOmega` clamp.
- `Lift`, `Drag` — magnitudes in the wind-aligned frame.
- `Force_X`, `Force_Y` — net body-frame force, written into `frame_a` after
  rotating into the world frame.
- `SpinRatio` — current ξ.
"""
component FlettnerRotor
  parameter R::Length = 2.5
  parameter H::Length = 24
  parameter MaxOmega::Real = 250

  parameter AirDensity::Density = 1.29

  "CFD-derived Cl(ξ) table (WaterLily SpinCyl, default = bundled sweep)"
  structural parameter cl_dataset::DyadData.DyadTimeseries = DyadData.DyadTimeseries(
    "dyad://DyadShip/flettner_coeffs.csv",
    independent_var = "xi",
    dependent_vars = ["Cl"]
  )
  "CFD-derived Cd(ξ) table (same CSV, Cd column)"
  structural parameter cd_dataset::DyadData.DyadTimeseries = DyadData.DyadTimeseries(
    "dyad://DyadShip/flettner_coeffs.csv",
    independent_var = "xi",
    dependent_vars = ["Cd"]
  )

  Omega_Order = RealInput()
  WindSpeedX_in = RealInput()
  WindSpeedY_in = RealInput()

  frame_a = Frame2D()

  Omega = RealOutput()
  Lift = RealOutput()
  Drag = RealOutput()
  Force_X = RealOutput()
  Force_Y = RealOutput()
  SpinRatio = RealOutput()

  # Cubic-spline lookup so the rhs Jacobian is smooth across the ξ-axis even
  # at the kinks between sweep points (raw WaterLily samples have natural CFD
  # scatter — linear interpolation produces a piecewise-discontinuous slope
  # that drives the adaptive stepper into dt < eps when the rotor saturates).
  Cl_lookup = BlockComponents.Tables.Interpolation(
    interpolation_type = BlockComponents.Tables.InterpolationType.CubicSpline(),
    extrapolation_type = BlockComponents.Tables.ExtrapolationType.Constant(),
    dataset = cl_dataset
  )
  Cd_lookup = BlockComponents.Tables.Interpolation(
    interpolation_type = BlockComponents.Tables.InterpolationType.CubicSpline(),
    extrapolation_type = BlockComponents.Tables.ExtrapolationType.Constant(),
    dataset = cd_dataset
  )

  variable U_app::Velocity
  variable xi::Real
  variable Cl::Real
  variable Cd::Real
  variable q::Real
  variable ex::Real
  variable ey::Real
  variable sign_omega::Real
  variable A_proj::Area
  variable omega_clamped::Real
relations
  omega_clamped = clamp(Omega_Order, -MaxOmega, MaxOmega)
  Omega = omega_clamped

  # Apparent wind magnitude (floored to avoid div-by-zero; force scales with U_app² so
  # the residual wrong direction at vanishing wind has zero force weight).
  U_app = sqrt(WindSpeedX_in^2 + WindSpeedY_in^2 + 1e-6)

  xi = abs(omega_clamped) * R / U_app
  SpinRatio = xi
  sign_omega = ifelse(omega_clamped >= 0, 1.0, -1.0)

  # CFD lookup. Cl_lookup/Cd_lookup share the same `xi` axis. The CSV stores
  # raw `WaterLily.total_force` coefficients (force on fluid). Negate to get
  # the engineering "force on body" convention used by the algebra below.
  Cl_lookup.u = xi
  Cd_lookup.u = xi
  Cl = -Cl_lookup.y
  Cd = -Cd_lookup.y

  # Dynamic pressure × projected area (diameter × rotor height).
  q = 0.5 * AirDensity * U_app^2
  A_proj = 2 * R * H

  Drag = q * A_proj * Cd
  Lift = q * A_proj * Cl

  # Wind direction unit vector in body frame.
  ex = WindSpeedX_in / U_app
  ey = WindSpeedY_in / U_app

  # Drag along wind direction; Magnus lift perpendicular, side chosen by ω sign
  # (positive ω = CCW from above ⇒ lift to the left of the wind vector).
  Force_X = Drag * ex - sign_omega * Lift * ey
  Force_Y = Drag * ey + sign_omega * Lift * ex

  # Body-frame forces → world frame (rotate by hull yaw). Same pattern as WingSail.
  frame_a.fx + cos(frame_a.phi) * Force_X - sin(frame_a.phi) * Force_Y = 0
  frame_a.fy + sin(frame_a.phi) * Force_X + cos(frame_a.phi) * Force_Y = 0
  frame_a.tau = 0
end
